## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to plan complex platform migrations under real constraints, balancing reliability, regulatory needs, and delivery velocity.

## Key Concepts
- Phased migration with pilots and dual write to reduce risk
- Dependency mapping and data contracts across squads
- Cutover strategy (canary/blue‑green) and rollback plan
- Observability, SLO enforcement, auditability, and data-retention alignment

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: determine rollout phase and guardrails
function migratePhase(phase) {
  if (phase === 'pilot') startPilot();
  else if (phase === 'staged') startStagedRollout();
  else if (phase === 'full') startFullCutover();
  else throw new Error('Unknown phase');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What metrics determine promotion from pilot to staged rollout?
- How would you mitigate Pulsar vendor lock-in risks and ensure exit paths?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates governance design capabilities for small remote teams, focusing on beginner-friendly processes that balance innovation with stability; emphasizes risk management, stakeholder communication, and scalable automation.

## Key Concepts
- Semantic versioning methodology
- Deprecation policy frameworks
- Automated compatibility testing
- Feature flag rollbacks
- Partner communication protocols

## Code Example
```javascript
// Version compatibility validator
function isCompatible(currentVersion, targetVersion) {
  const [cMA, cMi, cPa] = currentVersion.split('.').map(Number);
  const [tMA, tMi, tPa] = targetVersion.split('.').map(Number);

  // Major version changes are breaking
  if (cMA !== tMA) return false;

  // Minor version changes are backward compatible
  if (cMi > tMi) return false;

  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate onboarding into concrete artifacts, rituals, and measurable outcomes for a distributed setting.

## Key Concepts
- Onboarding playbooks that scale across time zones
- Clear artifacts and acceptance criteria
- Lightweight rituals that sustain momentum
- Quantifiable ramp metrics and experiments

## Code Example
```javascript
function rampScore({timeToFirstCommit, issuesClosed, prMergeTime}) {
  const t = timeToFirstCommit <= 7 ? 1 : 0;
  const i = issuesClosed >= 5 ? 1 : 0;
  const p = prMergeTime <= 48 ? 1 : 0;
  return t + i + p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate onboarding checklists and artifact generation?
- How would you adapt artifacts for contractors vs. full-time engineers?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to align cost, risk, and velocity in a multi-team, multi-region environment with shared data platform infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- FinOps and cost governance in cloud-native environments
- SLOs/SLIs and error budget policies
- Multi-tenancy isolation and privacy controls
- Budgeting, chargeback, and ownership models
- Incident costing and blameless postmortems
- Experiments to validate operational readiness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tagging for cost ownership
function tagResource(resource, owner, project) {
  return {
    ...resource,
    tags: {
      owner: owner,
      project: project,
      costCenter: getCostCenter(owner),
      environment: process.env.ENV
    }
  };
}
```
